1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:00,553
Welcome.

2
00:00:00,553 --> 00:00:05,949
In this video we walk through the AI Scaffolding and Agent
Architecture Guide.

3
00:00:05,949 --> 00:00:16,119
It is a handbook and starter kit that shows how to organize a project
so that AI coding agents work cheaply, reliably, and without wasting
context.

4
00:00:16,119 --> 00:00:17,088
Let's dive in.

5
00:00:17,888 --> 00:00:22,344
The core principle is to separate decision making from execution.

6
00:00:22,344 --> 00:00:32,286
Frontier models like Claude Opus or Gemini Pro cost twenty to forty
times more per output token than entry level models like Haiku or
Flash Lite.

7
00:00:32,286 --> 00:00:35,645
So the expensive models should write very little:

8
00:00:35,645 --> 00:00:39,142
they plan, design, and emit structured Work Orders.

9
00:00:39,142 --> 00:00:44,832
The cheap models do the heavy typing, writing code strictly driven by
those orders.

10
00:00:44,832 --> 00:00:50,180
And a task is dispatched by how well it is specified, not by how big
it looks.

11
00:00:50,980 --> 00:00:59,469
Loading whole documents destroys the context budget and causes
hallucinations, so retrieval is hierarchical and surgical.

12
00:00:59,469 --> 00:01:01,293
A session has five phases.

13
00:01:01,293 --> 00:01:01,643
Boot:

14
00:01:01,643 --> 00:01:04,099
load a root file under sixty lines.

15
00:01:04,099 --> 00:01:04,660
Routing:

16
00:01:04,660 --> 00:01:10,693
consult the single index, progress dot m d, which only points to the
right area sheet.

17
00:01:10,693 --> 00:01:11,184
Lookup:

18
00:01:11,184 --> 00:01:18,199
use grep to extract just the open tasks and rules, about one hundred
tokens instead of ten thousand.

19
00:01:18,199 --> 00:01:18,901
Execution:

20
00:01:18,901 --> 00:01:23,671
inspect specific symbols and run only the tests of the touched area.

21
00:01:23,671 --> 00:01:24,232
Closing:

22
00:01:24,232 --> 00:01:28,371
leave no trace by updating the state and running the audit.

23
00:01:29,171 --> 00:01:31,615
The architecture defines six roles.

24
00:01:31,615 --> 00:01:37,341
The planner, on Opus, is read only and turns vague work into verified
Work Orders.

25
00:01:37,341 --> 00:01:42,997
The coder, on Haiku, writes code from a locked order and makes no
design choices.

26
00:01:42,997 --> 00:01:47,536
The mechanic handles zero risk edits like renames and formatting.

27
00:01:47,536 --> 00:01:52,214
The light agent, on Sonnet, takes focused fixes that need judgment.

28
00:01:52,214 --> 00:01:58,917
The deep agent, on Opus at extra high effort, only accepts one
isolated bug with a reproduction.

29
00:01:58,917 --> 00:02:04,643
And the research agent explores many files and returns a five to ten
line summary.

30
00:02:05,443 --> 00:02:10,434
Before a task reaches an execution agent, the router asks five yes or
no questions.

31
00:02:10,434 --> 00:02:12,720
Are the exact files and symbols named?

32
00:02:12,720 --> 00:02:14,283
Is the diff deterministic?

33
00:02:14,283 --> 00:02:16,869
Is there an automated verification command?

34
00:02:16,869 --> 00:02:18,974
Does it touch three files or fewer?

35
00:02:18,974 --> 00:02:21,199
And are there zero pending decisions?

36
00:02:21,199 --> 00:02:23,665
Five yes answers means the task is ready.

37
00:02:23,665 --> 00:02:29,498
Any no sends it to the planner, which either makes it ready or splits
it into atomic Work Orders.

38
00:02:29,498 --> 00:02:32,686
Nothing goes straight to deep without a reproduction.

39
00:02:33,485 --> 00:02:36,390
Every agent finishes with exactly one status.

40
00:02:36,390 --> 00:02:38,650
DONE means the verification passed.

41
00:02:38,650 --> 00:02:42,975
BLOCKED means the spec was ambiguous, so it returns to the planner.

42
00:02:42,975 --> 00:02:47,558
FAILED times two means it failed twice, so the task escalates one
tier.

43
00:02:47,558 --> 00:02:50,334
The Work Order itself is a simple template:

44
00:02:50,334 --> 00:02:58,725
objective, target files and symbols, the required mutation, a
verification command, the expected result, and the designated agent.

45
00:02:58,725 --> 00:03:00,920
Nothing is left to interpretation.

46
00:03:01,720 --> 00:03:06,249
Documentation is split across three lifecycles to prevent context rot.

47
00:03:06,249 --> 00:03:13,107
Root guardians, CLAUDE dot m d and GEMINI dot m d, stay under sixty
lines and hold only global invariants.

48
00:03:13,107 --> 00:03:21,065
Living progress sheets, eighty to a hundred and twenty lines each,
describe the current state, open work and binding rules.

49
00:03:21,065 --> 00:03:27,469
Deep RFCs hold long term proposals and stay out of the live index so
they never inflate token cost.

50
00:03:28,269 --> 00:03:31,660
Part B is a copy and paste starter kit for any new project.

51
00:03:31,660 --> 00:03:32,866
You create this tree:

52
00:03:32,866 --> 00:03:48,209
the root guardian files, a dot claude folder with settings, the six
agents and three skills, a mirror of the skills for Antigravity, a
versioned pre commit hook, a CI workflow, the docs folder with the
single index, and two scripts, including the documentation audit.

53
00:03:48,209 --> 00:03:53,955
Then fill in the placeholders, commit, and hand the first real task to
the planner to test the gate.

54
00:03:54,755 --> 00:03:57,904
The audit script turns good habits into enforcement.

55
00:03:57,904 --> 00:04:00,327
It computes one number, lower is better:

56
00:04:00,327 --> 00:04:11,349
the tokens that are always loaded, plus the average sheet size, plus
penalties for things like an oversized root file, orphan sheets,
broken links, or a missing pointer to the index.

57
00:04:11,349 --> 00:04:19,949
The score can never rise above the saved baseline, a ratchet that is
checked by the pre commit hook, the Makefile, and continuous
integration.

58
00:04:20,750 --> 00:04:23,019
The guide supports both ecosystems.

59
00:04:23,019 --> 00:04:34,107
Claude Code reads CLAUDE dot m d and dot claude folders, while Google
Antigravity reads GEMINI dot m d and dot agents skills, with models
called pro, flash and flash lite.

60
00:04:34,107 --> 00:04:35,793
The parity rule is simple:

61
00:04:35,793 --> 00:04:42,471
GEMINI dot m d repeats the shared sections of CLAUDE dot m d verbatim
and swaps only the routing table.

62
00:04:42,471 --> 00:04:48,696
Both share the same index, so any team member can use either assistant
with the same guarantees.

63
00:04:49,496 --> 00:04:54,293
Part D lists thirteen baseline rules, each with a way to enforce it.

64
00:04:54,293 --> 00:04:54,999
One index.

65
00:04:54,999 --> 00:04:55,845
A thin root.

66
00:04:55,845 --> 00:04:57,327
State, not chronicle.

67
00:04:57,327 --> 00:04:58,244
Small sheets.

68
00:04:58,244 --> 00:04:59,302
Surgical reads.

69
00:04:59,302 --> 00:05:00,642
Route by readiness.

70
00:05:00,642 --> 00:05:01,771
Explicit status.

71
00:05:01,771 --> 00:05:03,393
Mandatory verification.

72
00:05:03,393 --> 00:05:04,451
Leave no trace.

73
00:05:04,451 --> 00:05:05,298
The ratchet.

74
00:05:05,298 --> 00:05:07,203
A bounded improvement loop.

75
00:05:07,203 --> 00:05:09,531
Repeated workflows become skills.

76
00:05:09,531 --> 00:05:13,269
And every costly surprise adds one line to the rules.

77
00:05:13,269 --> 00:05:17,784
That is how the scaffolding improves itself instead of decaying.

78
00:05:18,584 --> 00:05:19,249
To sum up:

79
00:05:19,249 --> 00:05:30,290
let top models decide and cheap models execute, route by readiness,
keep one index with a thin root and small sheets, verify everything,
and enforce it with an audit.

80
00:05:30,290 --> 00:05:34,414
The full guide, with every template, is in scaffold dot p d f.

81
00:05:34,414 --> 00:05:37,806
Thanks for watching, and see you in the next video.

